#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.14"
# dependencies = []
# ///
"""Name a tmux session after an agent session.

Only an agent tmux itself was told to run names anything: the pane must
have been started with an agent launcher, so an agent typed at a shell
prompt leaves that session's name alone. The name describes what tmux
was opened to do, not whatever is running in it at the moment.

Among the agents that qualify, the first to name a session owns it
until the session ends, and one started inside another's pane leaves
the name alone. A session carries one name, so an agent launched as the
command of a second pane still moves that name, now through the owner's
slot and format. To hand a session to a different agent, unset its
owner with `tmux set-option -u @agent_namer` and start that agent as a
pane's own command, since the prompt route is refused either way.

A muse, pi, or opencode run takes the session name as its argument and
renames once per run: Muse sets no pane title to follow, pi's carries
the working directory, and opencode's is prefixed and truncated, so
muse's skill, pi's extension, and opencode's plugin each hand the
current name over at session start and after every rename. An empty
name renames to the bare agent and slot -- the form a session keeps
until a real name exists, which is how pi and opencode sessions first
name themselves.

A muse-hook run answers a PreToolUse hook: it approves with the tool
input echoed back and, only when the session carries a name it has not
reported yet, prepends a silent rename to shell calls (which the agent
executes with its own tmux environment, declaring the tmux socket
grant the sandboxed call needs) or adds a directive for the agent to
rename through a muse run, since the hook environment itself carries
no tmux address.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import functools
import json
import os
import re
import select
import shlex
import socket
import sqlite3
import subprocess
import sys
import time
from enum import StrEnum
from typing import IO, cast

